data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR-Cas9 uses a guide RNA to find target DNA, and Cas9 cuts the genome at the desired location [7].\n- After the cut, the cell may repair it with errors that switch off or break the gene, or researchers can supply a DNA template to add a new function [3].\n- Scientists commonly use CRISPR to \"knock out\" genes, and can replace a disease-causing mutation with a healthy sequence [2].\n- It is used in biotechnology, synthetic biology, and basic research; one platform, CRISPR SWAPnDROP, enables genome editing in *E. coli* and large-scale DNA transfer between bacterial species [4]."}

